{"from": "artifact:vardar-gate-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-06-politarchs", "grade": "A", "note": "One of the earliest extant inscriptions using the title politarches stood on the Vardar Gate of Thessalonica, the very city of Acts 17:6 and 17:8 - the office title Luke uses is attested in stone for exactly this city."}
{"from": "artifact:vardar-gate-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "place:thessalonica", "grade": "A", "note": "The inscription was found on the Vardar Gate at Thessalonica; the source names the city and the gate directly."}
{"from": "site:areopagus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-19-areopagus-institution", "grade": "B", "note": "The Areopagus was a principal council of Athens that 'continued to function as a body of former archons in Roman times' and was 'one of the most prestigious and politically powerful institutions in Athens' after Sulla - the same institution in the same city in Paul's period."}
{"from": "site:areopagus", "type": "attests", "to": "place:athens", "grade": "B", "note": "The Areopagus hill is a documented site of Athens, northwest of the Acropolis; the source names the city and location."}
{"from": "site:areopagus", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-17-22-stood-areopagus", "note": "By the time of Paul's visit the Areopagus Council 'likely would not have met on the actual Areopagus hill by the time of Paul's visit, but rather in the agora or in the Stoa Basileios', and it is unclear whether Acts means the council in session or the physical hill."}
{"from": "text:pausanias-1-1-4", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-23-altar-unknown-god", "grade": "B", "note": "Pausanias, describing the road from Phalerum to Athens, records 'altars of the gods named Unknown' at Athens - the same custom of inscribing altars to unknown gods in exactly this city, reported independently of Acts."}
{"from": "text:diogenes-laertius-1-110", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-23-altar-unknown-god", "grade": "B", "note": "Diogenes Laertius reports Epimenides' plague atonement at Athens: 'altars may be found in different parts of Attica with no name inscribed upon them' - nameless altars in Attica, a tradition from the archaic period."}
{"from": "text:pausanias-1-1-4", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-16-idols", "grade": "C", "note": "Pausanias (c. 150 CE, a century after Paul) lists in Athens temple after temple, sanctuary after sanctuary, altars and images - the idol-dense city of 17:16, though from a later date."}
{"from": "artifact:pergamon-unknown-gods-altar", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-23-altar-unknown-god", "grade": "D", "note": "The identification of the altar's reading is disputed: the stone found at Pergamum in 1909 is mutilated and the restoration 'To unknown gods' is conjectural, quoted only from Barton's 1911 handbook via a modern blog; and Pergamum is not Athens."}
{"from": "text:aratus-phaenomena", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-28-aratus-offspring", "grade": "A", "note": "Aratus's Phaenomena, line 5, reads 'For we are also his offspring' in a real pre-Christian Greek poem (Stoic-influenced, 3rd century BCE) - Paul's 'as some of your own poets have said' cites a documented text and line."}
{"from": "text:epimenides-cretica", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-28-in-him-live", "grade": "D", "note": "The attribution of 'in him we live and move and have our being' to Epimenides is disputed: the line survives only in Rendel Harris's reconstruction from a Nestorian Syriac commentary on Acts 17:28, not in any manuscript of Epimenides."}
{"from": "site:via-egnatia", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-01-route-thessalonica", "grade": "B", "note": "The Via Egnatia ran to 'the northern coastline of the Aegean Sea at Thessalonica' with Amphipolis among its stations, and Apollonia (Mygdonia) stood 'on the route between Amphipolis and Thessalonica' - the itinerary of 17:1 on the documented Roman road."}
{"from": "site:agora-of-athens", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-18-philosophers", "grade": "C", "note": "Both schools were Athenian institutions: Stoicism 'was founded in the ancient Agora of Athens by Zeno of Citium' and 'flourished throughout the Greco-Roman world until the 3rd century CE'; Epicurus's school was the Garden in Athens - schools present in Paul's Athens, though the individual encounter is unrecorded elsewhere."}
{"from": "site:amphipolis", "type": "attests", "to": "place:amphipolis", "grade": "A", "note": "Amphipolis is a documented, excavated ancient city in Macedonia; the source names it and records excavated remains."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-42-43", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-07-caesar-decrees", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus reports that all the people of the Jews gave assurance of their good-will to Caesar and the king's government by oath, only the Pharisees refusing - loyalty-oaths to Caesar as a known obligation in exactly this era; not the accusers' charge itself."}
{"from": "artifact:paphlagonian-oath-stele", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-17-07-caesar-decrees", "grade": "C", "note": "The Paphlagonian oath stele (OGIS 532, 3 BCE) records provincials and Roman traders swearing an oath of loyalty to Caesar Augustus - emperor-loyalty oaths are a documented imperial practice, but this is Paphlagonia in 3 BCE, not a decree at Thessalonica in c. 50 CE."}
